            <fr:route>/QH5J/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="POTW: 🎂 CAKE: Compiler-Agent Co-Design for Frontier Kernel Evolution"><fr:link href="/005G/" title="Papers of a week" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/005G/" display-uri="005G" type="local">POTW</fr:link>: 🎂 <fr:link href="/yeCAKECompilerAgentCoDesign2026/" title="CAKE: Compiler-Agent Co-Design for Frontier Kernel Evolution" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/yeCAKECompilerAgentCoDesign2026/" display-uri="yeCAKECompilerAgentCoDesign2026" type="local">CAKE: Compiler-Agent Co-Design for Frontier Kernel Evolution</fr:link></fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>19</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="TLDR">TLDR</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Problem.</html:strong> In this paper, they aim to address the problem of <html:em>agentic kernel generation</html:em> or as we refer to it: <fr:link href="/AV36/" title="Vibe compilers" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AV36/" display-uri="AV36" type="local"><html:em>vibe compilation</html:em></fr:link>. Specifically, they target <fr:link href="/OGCS4/" title="CUDA Hierarchy" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCS4/" display-uri="OGCS4" type="local">CUDA</fr:link> and raw PTX as a backend.</html:p>
<html:figure><html:img width="90%" src="/bafkrmihh67pzjirkpud4lemsq3uzet4b7t7s6eq4sssbbubuzwvj3xkphm.png" /></html:figure>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Technical challenges.</html:strong> There are many issues that arise in <fr:link href="/AV36/" title="Vibe compilers" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AV36/" display-uri="AV36" type="local">vibe compilation</fr:link>. A non-exhaustive list of challenges is as follows:</html:p>
<html:ul><html:li><html:em><fr:link href="/FN71/" title="Reward Hacking" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/FN71/" display-uri="FN71" type="local">Reward Hacking</fr:link></html:em>: A very common theme for any reinforcement learning based approach. In particular, see <fr:link href="/langeRobustAgenticCUDA2025/" title="Towards Robust Agentic CUDA Kernel Benchmarking, Verification, and Optimization" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/langeRobustAgenticCUDA2025/" display-uri="langeRobustAgenticCUDA2025" type="local">this paper</fr:link> for some examples of agents hacking <fr:link href="/ouyangKernelBenchCanLLMs2025/" title="KernelBench: Can LLMs Write Efficient GPU Kernels?" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/ouyangKernelBenchCanLLMs2025/" display-uri="ouyangKernelBenchCanLLMs2025" type="local">KernelBench</fr:link>.</html:li>
  <html:li><html:em>Synchronization errors</html:em>: Even given a list of input output examples to check against, we cannot possibly determine whether there is an underlying race condition that appears rarely enough to pass the checks.</html:li>
  <html:ul><html:li>This seems to be a main focus of things like <fr:link href="/TIRx2026/" title="TIRx: An Open Compiler Stack for Evolving Frontier ML Kernels " uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/TIRx2026/" display-uri="TIRx2026" type="local">TIRx</fr:link>, where they hope to check these things at the IR level.</html:li></html:ul></html:ul>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>19</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Contributions">Contributions</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Core contribution.</html:strong> They develop <html:em>CAKE IR</html:em>, which is an intermediate representation for writing CUDA kernels. More importantly, they have the <html:strong>agent</html:strong> <html:em>co-design</html:em> the IR while trying to generate kernels. This is an iterative process, where the agent must implement existing kernels in its own designed IR. To do so, requires adding necessary features to the IR like <fr:link href="/JKNL/" title="Warp Specialization" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JKNL/" display-uri="JKNL" type="local">Warp Specialization</fr:link> along with the corresponding lowering passes.</html:p>
<html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmiclychzgexsdmq2yad2laagwguagrhnq2jykmpww2pknrcx5p6r4u.png" />
<html:figcaption>Starting bare IR. The agent is then given a warp-specialized kernel and sees that it needs to add support for warp specialization via <html:code>lm.role</html:code></html:figcaption></html:figure>
<html:p>They can then iteratively build on this pipeline adding architecture specific features. For example, the IR example they give shows that they have an <html:code>lm.tmem</html:code> API for <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">TMEM</fr:link>. This can be thought of as a v3 given below.</html:p>
<html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmidniogk5alcxsytvbm2xsz4qgwiez473klwgamvmpp7hn46v7o7qa.png" />
<html:figcaption>Building on the IR with <fr:link href="/JKNL/" title="Warp Specialization" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/JKNL/" display-uri="JKNL" type="local">Warp Specialization</fr:link>. Add support for architecture specific features like <fr:link href="/YCAO/" title="Tensor Memory (TMEM)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/YCAO/" display-uri="YCAO" type="local">TMEM</fr:link> as the candles on top of the IR</html:figcaption></html:figure>
<html:p>How this is actually implemented is a trade secret<html:label class="sidenote-ref"><html:input type="checkbox" /></html:label><html:span class="sidenote">I suspect one reason that a lot of this is not publicly available is that it is only understood by GPT 5.6</html:span>.</html:p>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Evaluation.</html:strong> They evaluate against (their own) well-tuned kernels from <fr:link href="/yeFlashInferEfficientCustomizable2025/" title="FlashInfer: Efficient and Customizable Attention Engine for LLM Inference Serving" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/yeFlashInferEfficientCustomizable2025/" display-uri="yeFlashInferEfficientCustomizable2025" type="local">FlashInfer</fr:link>.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>19</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Limitations">Limitations</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Assumptions.</html:strong></html:p>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Where they might struggle.</html:strong> It is unclear how well this would work for ASICs like <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPUs</fr:link> or <fr:link href="/V5KP/" title="Trainium" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/V5KP/" display-uri="V5KP" type="local">Trainium</fr:link>. I suspect that in order to "converge" to a good IR, you need to reduce the degrees of freedom through a large number of kernel examples. The kernel ecosystem of these accelerators is far less mature, making it much harder to extract useful abstractions. </html:p>
<html:p>I believe the layering analogy was meant as adding increasingly specific features, but I wonder whether a vibe <fr:link href="/IR7B/" title="MLIR" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/IR7B/" display-uri="IR7B" type="local">MLIR</fr:link> could be developed to allow other accelerators to inherit some of the benefits.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>19</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Outstanding Questions">Outstanding Questions</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>Most of the work in this paper is not publicly available, so I have a lot of outstanding questions.</html:p>

  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(115, 73, 35);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(115, 73, 35);">Question.</html:span> What are the exact input / outputs in IR developement and during search?</html:div>


<html:p>For the IR, they have "IR principles"</html:p>
<html:ol><html:li>Program safety</html:li>
<html:li>Hardware conformance</html:li>
<html:li>Data consistency</html:li>
<html:li>Schedule semantics</html:li>
<html:li>Numerical validation</html:li>
<html:li>Performance analysis</html:li>
<html:li>Optimization guidance</html:li></html:ol>
<html:p>My best guess is that the workflow is roughly 1. prepend these principles to the input via some prompt 2. provide new kernel(s) that likely require some new abstraction and 3. have GPT vibe code IR updates. </html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
</fr:mainmatter>
